Subject: [PATCH 313/313] CLAUDE.md -- the 0308-0312 flashes, and the fault
 that keeps happening

Two sections. The second is the board record for four runs; the first is
the reason they are worth reading together.

FIVE FAULTS IN THIS SERIES WERE THE SAME FAULT. Not the same code, the
same shape: a value read to answer a question it does not answer, where
the reading is plausible and the wrong answer accuses something else.
NETSTREAM_IDLE, stations_load()'s return, STREAM_CODEC_NONE,
uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark(), and a phase timer against a 30 s limit.
Tabulated with what each was asked and what each actually means, because
the fifth was found by recognising the pattern rather than by
instrumenting -- the first time on this project that reading this file
beat reflashing.

Two tells are written down as rules. A log that accuses something you
cannot see: suspect the value that named it -- 0203 and 0311 both read as
a station hanging up, and both times it was us. And the evidence is
usually in the figure: 2068 bytes of worst-ever free is proof the task
survived the 17744-byte call it was refused for, and 30025 ms against a
30000 ms limit is proof the stream died 25 ms after it succeeded. There
is a third instance in this file's own opening paragraph, which cost two
panics.

The board record notes that the order of the four runs was not optional:
0308's routing fault made 0310's unreadable, and 0310's permanent refusal
made 0311 unreachable.

Also recorded, because they were open questions and are now answered: ICY
titles work and the two silent stations are simply silent, so the demuxer
was never the problem; zeno.fm's handshake failures are the station, and
it earns its place in the list by being the only thing that exercises the
retry path; and the AAC decoder-open cost spreads 4 KB across runs, which
the internal-RAM budget treats as fixed.

The open list leads with the cheapest unknown in the project: the screen
has still never been looked at. Four runs of faults found in logs, while
0301's badge and status line have been sitting on a working stream for
minutes with nobody saying what they looked like.
